    /* AP2 OFF (29 Aug 2026): Aurora dormant, kept for restoration */ // h+=auroraToggle('1.3-orch','<p style="margin:0 0 8px"><strong>Aurora Windows — Integrations.</strong> Most of Aurora\'s connections turn out to be fragile.</p><ul style="margin:0 0 8px;padding-left:20px"><li style="margin-bottom:5px">A nightly ERP→HubSpot sync nobody owns.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px">A manual CSV the Sales team exports by hand from the ERP into its quoting spreadsheet.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px">The IT manager\'s <em>personal</em> API key wiring production scheduling straight into procurement — if he leaves, the key and the connection leave with him.</li></ul><p style="margin:8px 0 0">Fragile connections are the routes a failure or an attacker takes first — which is what makes them the priority to fix.</p>');
    h+='<div style="padding:12px 16px;background:rgba(90,125,138,.04);border-left:3px solid var(--gold);margin-bottom:20px;font-size:14px;color:var(--stone);line-height:1.6"><strong style="color:var(--paper)">What counts as an integration?</strong> An integration is any connection between two systems that moves data or triggers actions. This includes automated file transfers, API calls, database links, manual CSV exports, email-based workflows, and custom scripts. Many organizations have integrations that someone set up years ago and has since left. Such integrations are often undocumented and ungoverned. AI systems will use these connections, and if they are fragile, AI can make the failures they cause larger and faster.</div>';
